 Here’s a step-by-step solution to the problem:

1. Melanie has already saved 10 toothpicks per week for 12 weeks, so she currently has:

 10 toothpicks/week * 12 weeks = \boxed{\textbf{120 toothpicks}}

2. She still needs 200 toothpicks - 120 toothpicks = \boxed{\textbf{80 toothpicks}}

3. To find out how many more weeks it will take her to collect 80 toothpicks at a rate of 10 toothpicks per week, we divide the number of toothpicks she needs by the number of toothpicks she saves per week:

 80 toothpicks / 10 toothpicks/week = \boxed{\textbf{8 weeks}}

So, Melanie needs 8 more weeks to collect 200 toothpicks for her toothpick sculpture.

 Here's how you can solve it:
\begin{aligned}
  \text{Area of northeast wall:} & 10ft \times 8ft = 80\text{ square feet} \\
  \text{Area of northwest wall:} & 10ft \times 8ft = 80\text{ square feet} \\
  \text{Area of southeast wall:} & 5ft \times 8ft = 40\text{ square feet} \\
  \text{Area of southwest wall:} & 5ft \times 8ft = 40\text{ square feet} \\
  \text{Total area to be painted:} & 2 \times 80 + 2 \times 40 = 160 + 80 = \boxed{240\text{ square feet}}
\end{aligned}

Now, let's find out how many gallons of paint are needed:
\begin{aligned}
  \text{Gallons of paint needed:} & \text{Total area} / \text{Area covered by 1 gallon}  \\
  & = 240\text{ square feet} / 20\text{ square feet/gallon} = \boxed{12\text{ gallons}}
\end{aligned}

Finally, let's find out the cost:
\begin{aligned}
  \text{Cost:} & \text{Number of gallons} \times \text{Cost per gallon} \\
  & = 12 \times 12 = 144 \times 1 = \boxed{$\$$144}
\end{aligned}

Therefore, the answer is \boxed{$144$}, which means it will cost $144 to paint the room.
